Phase 6 was too large to do in one go, so I split it into four parts. The first two are now done: 6a (security) and 6b (cluster DNS). Everything passes: 120 unit/integration tests, 39/39 kubectl command tests, and 59/59 end-to-end checks against a real secure cluster. fmt and clippy are clean, and no test processes, containers or firewall rules were left behind.

Security (6a)
Certificates: a new kubeadm tool creates the cluster CA, a certificate for each component, and kubeconfigs (admin.conf, kubelet.conf, …). kubeadm kubeconfig user --client-name jane --org dev makes a login for a new user.
API server: now HTTPS only. You log in with a client certificate, a static token, or a ServiceAccount token; with nothing, you're anonymous and can only read health endpoints. Each component uses its own identity.
RBAC:
Ships with the standard default roles (cluster-admin, admin, edit, view, …).
Users cannot grant permissions they don't already hold.
kubectl auth can-i (including --list), auth whoami, --as impersonation and create role/rolebinding all work.
Error messages match upstream Kubernetes exactly.
ServiceAccount tokens:
kubectl create token works.
Every pod automatically gets a token, the CA certificate and its namespace mounted in.
The kubelet renews the token before it expires.
Deleting the pod or the ServiceAccount invalidates its tokens immediately.
Kubelet: its API was previously open to anyone; it now requires HTTPS plus a login, and checks permissions with the API server. kubectl logs/exec reach it over TLS.
Cluster DNS (6b)
The real CoreDNS image runs as an add-on at 10.96.0.10. Pods get proper DNS settings, so web, web.default.svc.cluster.local and external names all resolve. CoreDNS itself is a real Kubernetes client: it talks to our API server from inside the cluster with its own token, under RBAC. That makes it a good proof that all of 6a works together.

Bugs found and fixed
RBAC role names like system:node were rejected, because names were checked as DNS names. Fixed with a per-resource name check, as upstream does.
Plain-HTTP tools (scheduler, kubemark, rkubectl, tests) crashed at startup. The HTTP library now needs a crypto provider even without TLS; it's installed everywhere now.
The e2e test occasionally failed when its random port was already in use. It now picks a free range of ports.
What changed for you
hack/local-up-cluster.sh now starts a secure cluster by default. The API server listens on https://172.17.0.1:6443 (the Docker bridge address, so pods can reach it), and _output/local/kubeconfig is the admin login. SECURE=0 gives the old insecure HTTP mode. Commands to try are in the new "3c. Security and DNS" section of docs/testing-guide.md; the design is in docs/phase-6-security.md.

Still not done
No per-node permission limits yet: a kubelet's credentials can read every Secret in the cluster.
No certificate rotation or certificate-request API; certificates last 1 year.
No audit logging.
system:kube-controller-manager has full access, because the controller manager runs under a single identity rather than one per controller.
Next is 6c: swapping our in-memory store for real etcd behind the existing storage interface. After that, 6d: containerd, kubectl explain, metrics and audit.
